  <h2>Why AmazonSmile ended</h2>
  <p>
    AmazonSmile ran from 2013 until <strong>February 20, 2023</strong>. It donated about
    <strong>{AMAZONSMILE.ratePercent}% of eligible purchases</strong> to a charity you selected, and
    gave roughly <strong>${AMAZONSMILE.lifetimeMillions} million</strong> globally over its lifetime.
    Amazon closed it, saying the spread-thin impact per organization didn’t justify the program — which
    left millions of shoppers and thousands of nonprofits looking for a replacement.
  </p>

  <h2>The best AmazonSmile alternatives in 2026</h2>
  <p>
    Several services let you raise money for a nonprofit when you shop. Most work like AmazonSmile did —
    a small percentage of your spending goes to a cause, at no cost to you:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>ShopRaise</strong>, <strong>Givebacks</strong>, <strong>iGive</strong> — browser/app shopping portals that pass a share of online purchases to a nonprofit.</li>
    <li><strong>RaiseRight</strong> — a gift-card program where a percentage of each card funds your organization.</li>
    <li><strong>eBay for Charity</strong> — donate a portion of a sale or purchase through eBay.</li>
    <li><strong>Good Circles</strong> — local-first: you save about 10% at local businesses, and ~10% of the merchant’s profit funds a nonprofit you choose.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    See the full, sourced side-by-side on our
    <a href="/amazonsmile-alternative/">best AmazonSmile alternatives comparison</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>How Good Circles compares</h2>
  <p>
    The difference with a local-first model is that the money works twice. On every <strong>$100</strong>
    you spend locally through Good Circles, about <strong>{dollars(PER_100.shopperSaves)} comes back to you</strong>,
    about <strong>{dollars(PER_100.nonprofit)} funds a nonprofit</strong> you choose (from the merchant’s
    profit), and about <strong>${PER_100.localRecirculation} stays circulating in your local economy</strong> —
    versus roughly <strong>${PER_100.chainRecirculation}</strong> when the same $100 goes to a national chain
    (Civic Economics).
  </p>
    <p>
